{"text":"making to give Sonnv a decent home and how httle he appreciated it.\n\nSonnv didn't play the piano that day. By evening, Isabel's mother had calmed down but then there was the old man to deal with, and Isabel herself. Isabel says she did her best to be calm but she broke down and started crying. She says she just watched Sonny's face. She could tell, by watching him, what was happening with him. And what was happening was that they penetrated his cloud, they had reached him. E\\en if their fingers had been a thousand times more gentle than human fingers ever are, he could hardly help feeling that thev had stripped him naked and were spitting on that nakedness. For he also had to see that his presence, that music, which was life or death to him, had been torture for them and that thev had endured it, not at all for his sake, but only for mine. And Sonny couldn't take that. He can take it a little better today than he could then but he's still not ver\\- good at it and, frankly. I don't know anvbodv who is.\n\nThe silence of the next few davs must have been louder than the sound of all the music ever played since time began. One morning, before she went to work, Isabel was in his room for something and she suddenly realized that all of his records were gone. And she knew for certain that he was gone. And he was. He went as far as the navy would carrv him. He finally sent me a postcard from some place in Greece and that was the first I knew that Sonny was still alive. I didn't see him anv more until we were both back in New York and the war had long been over.\n\nHe was a man bv then, of course, but I wasn't willing to see it. He came by the house from time to time, but we fought almost every time we met. I didn't like the way he carried himself, loose and dreamlike all the time, and I didn't like his friends, and his music seemed to be merely an excuse for the life he led. It sounded just that weird and disordered.\n\nThen we had a fight, a prettv awful fight, and I didn't see him for months. By and by I looked him up, where he was living, in a furnished room in the Village, and I tried to make it up. But there were lots of other people in the room and Sonnv just lay on his bed. and he wouldn't come downstairs with me. and he treated these other people as though they were his family and I weren't. So I got mad and then he got mad. and then I told him that he might just as well be dead as li\\e the wav he was li\\ing. Tlien he stood up and he told me not to worry about him anv more in life, that he uus dead as far as I was concerned. Tlien he pushed me to the door and the other people looked on as though nothing were hapixning. and he slammed the door behind me. I stood in the hallway, staring at the door. I heard somebody laugh in the room and then the tears came to my eyes. I started down the steps, whistling to keep from crying.","voice":"en-US-AriaNeural","words":[[0.1,0.5,0,6],[0.5125,0.6,7,9],[0.6125,0.825,10,14],[0.8375,1.1625,15,20],[1.175,1.25,21,22],[1.2625,1.6125,23,29],[1.625,1.95,30,34],[1.9625,2.075,35,38],[2.0875,2.25,39,42],[2.2625,2.575,43,48],[2.5875,2.7125,49,51],[2.725,3.3875,52,63],[3.4,3.7125,64,66],[4.575,4.9875,69,74],[5.0,5.2875,75,81],[5.3,5.5125,82,86],[5.525,5.625,87,90],[5.6375,6.05,91,96],[6.0625,6.3,97,101],[6.3125,6.675,102,105],[7.55,7.775,107,109],[7.7875,8.275,110,117],[8.45,8.925,119,127],[8.9375,9.2375,128,134],[9.25,9.425,135,138],[9.4375,9.7875,139,145],[9.8,10.1375,146,150],[10.15,10.3125,151,154],[10.325,10.5,155,159],[10.5125,10.65,160,165],[10.6625,10.7875,166,169],[10.8,10.9,170,173],[10.9125,11.1375,174,177],[11.15,11.375,178,181],[11.3875,11.475,182,184],[11.4875,11.7625,185,189],[11.775,12.05,190,194],[12.2125,12.4125,196,199],[12.425,12.8375,200,206],[12.85,13.4625,207,214],[14.325,14.8125,216,222],[14.825,15.075,223,227],[15.0875,15.225,228,231],[15.2375,15.4125,232,235],[15.425,15.5625,236,239],[15.575,15.8375,240,244],[15.85,15.925,245,247],[15.9375,16.0625,248,250],[16.